Good vening everyone , today we are going to discuss about big data fundamentals and databricks . It's all about theoretical part of it 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 1: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Three modules,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Let\rquote s take a quick look at what are the main topics we are going to cover today . 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Big data fundamentals , Introduction to databricks , Databricks Architechture}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par We\rquote ll start with Big Data itself: what it means, why it matters, and why conventional approaches sometimes struggle with its scale, speed, and variety.
\par Once that foundation is clear, we\rquote ll introduce Databricks and see how the Lakehouse brings engineering, analytics, machine learning, and AI together.
\par Finally, we\rquote ll go behind the scenes and explore how Databricks works, including Spark, compute, Delta Lake, layers in dabataricks and Unity Catalog.
\par So, we\rquote ll move step by step from the problem, to the platform, and finally to the architecture.\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 2:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par In the next set of slides we will talk about }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 big data fundamentals }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Why Big data?}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par Think about how much data we create every day through our phones, applications, online purchases, smart devices, factory sensors, and business systems.
\par Across the world, we\rquote re producing hundreds of millions of terabytes of data every day. Billions of connected devices are also generating data continuously, often in real time.
\par But collecting data alone doesn\rquote t create value. The important question is: can we store it, understand it, trust it, and use it quickly enough?
\par Organizations that can do that are better positioned to understand their customers, improve operations, make faster decisions, and build modern AI applications.
\par That is the real reason Big Data matters.\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \~
\par \~
\par Slide3:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 what the term \lquote Big Data\rquote  actually means.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \'93Now that we understand why it matters, let\rquote s clarify }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 what the term \lquote Big Data\rquote  actually means.\'94}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 It doesn\rquote t simply mean having a very large file. Data becomes \lquote big\rquote 
 when our existing systems can no longer store, process, or analyse it efficiently enough for the required business outcome.
\par There are three simple ways to understand this slide.
\par The data might be too large for one machine. It might arrive too quickly for occasional batch processing. Or it might come in too many different formats, such as tables, JSON, text, images, and video.
\par In practice, we often experience all three challenges together.
\par Why does this matter? Because buying one increasingly powerful server is not a sustainable solution. Instead, we distribute the storage and processing across multiple resources.
\par This is the problem that modern platforms such as Databricks are designed to address.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide4:}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 In this slide we will see the five v's of big data }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93A simple way to understand Big Data is through the five Vs shown here.
\par Firstly ,  we have }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Volume}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 : how much data are we dealing with?
\par Next is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Velocity}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 : how quickly is that data arriving, and how soon we need to process it?
\par Then there is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Variety}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
. Data no longer comes only in neat tables. It can also be JSON, logs, text, images, audio, or video.
\par The fourth V is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Veracity}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
, which asks whether we can actually trust the data. Is it accurate, complete, and consistent, or does it contain errors and noise?
\par The final V is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Value}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
. Even if we have enormous amounts of fast-moving data, it is useful only when it helps us make decisions or achieve a meaningful result.
\par This slide matters because Big Data is not simply about size. It is about managing all five of these challenges together.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 5:conventional vsBig Data}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide compares conventional and Big Data processing approaches.
\par Conventional systems primarily process predictable, structured data using relational databases and predefined schemas.
\par Big Data systems support larger and more diverse datasets using distributed, parallel processing.
\par They are primarily designed to scale out across multiple resources and support flexible schema strategies.
\par The distinction is not absolute. Both approaches can use distributed infrastructure, schemas, and SQL.
\par The comparison explains why technologies such as Spark and the Databricks Lakehouse are used for modern data workloads.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 6: challenges of big data . }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide presents six common Big Data challenges.
\par Big Data sounds powerful, but working with big data is not automatically easy.
\par Data must be stored reliably and affordably.
\par Processing must meet business performance requirements.
\par Different systems, formats, and sources must be integrated.
\par Data quality must be maintained so analytics and AI remain trustworthy.
\par With respect to additional attributes like Access, privacy, governance, and compliance must be controlled.
\par Finally, organizations must manage technical skills, operational complexity, and cost.
\par These challenges explain the need for a scalable, governed platform such as Databricks.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 7: Big data ecosystem }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide shows the journey that data takes before it becomes useful.
\par We start on the left with the places where data is created: applications, databases, sensors, IoT devices, and system logs.
\par Next, we need to bring that data into the platform. This is ingestion, and it can happen in scheduled batches or continuously through streaming.
\par Once it arrives, we store it in a scalable location, such as cloud storage using Delta Lake.
\par Then comes processing. This is where we use Spark or SQL to clean the data, remove duplicates, combine sources, and prepare it for use.
\par After that, analysts, data scientists, and machine-learning teams can explore the data and produce insights.
\par Finally, those insights reach users through dashboards, reports, applications, or models.
\par The first three are about capturing and moving the data. The last three are about refining it and using it. Same flow is followed, almost every platform."
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 8: Big data in real world \line Big data used in some of the industry like retail , finance, healthcaree , and manufacturing industryu }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93So far, we\rquote ve discussed Big Data as a technical concept. This slide shows what it looks like in everyday business.
\par Retail uses customer, sales, and inventory data for recommendations and demand forecasting.
\par Financial institutions use transaction data for fraud detection, credit risk, and regulatory reporting.
\par Healthcare and life sciences use clinical, claims, device, and genomic data for patient insights, population health, and drug discovery.
\par Manufacturers use sensor and operational data for predictive maintenance, quality improvement, and supply-chain optimization.
\par The use cases differ, but each requires scalable processing, trusted data, analytics, AI, and governance.
\par These are core capabilities provided by Databricks.\'94
\par \~
\par In the next set of slides we will talk about some of the concepts involved in databricks
\par Slide9: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 what is databricks}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93We\rquote ve discussed the challenges of Big Data. Now let\rquote s look at the platform designed to help address many of them.
\par Databricks gives data engineers, analysts, data scientists, machine-learning engineers, and governance teams a shared environment for working with data and AI.
\par The four boxes show the main idea.
\par Data engineers can ingest, clean, and transform data through reliable pipelines.
\par Analysts can use SQL for reporting, dashboards, and business intelligence.
\par Data scientists and AI teams can explore data and build, track, and deploy models.
\par At the same time, governance helps ensure that people have appropriate access and that data remains discoverable, traceable, and controlled.
\par Why does this matter? Without a unified platform, these activities often happen in separate tools and create duplicated data and disconnected workflows.
\par Databricks brings those teams and workloads together around the same trusted data.\'94
\par \~
\par \~
\par Slide 11: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Lakehouse : Best of both worlds}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93To understand Databricks, we first need to understand the Lakehouse.
\par A data warehouse is very good at organizing trusted, structured data for SQL, reporting, and dashboards. 
\par But it was not originally designed for every kind of raw data or machine-learning workload.
\par A data lake takes the opposite approach. It can store almost any data at a large scale and relatively low cost. 
\par But without good management, it can become difficult to trust, organize, and query.
\par The Lakehouse brings those two ideas together.
\par It keeps the flexibility and openness of a data lake while adding the reliability and management features we expect from a warehouse. Delta Lake helps provide capabilities such as ACID transactions, schema enforcement, and version history.
\par Why this slide is important?  engineers, and data scientists can work from a shared data foundation instead of constantly moving data between separate lake and warehouse platforms.\'94
\par \~
\par Slide 12: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 One platform , every data persona }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide shows four personas supported by Databricks.
\par Data engineers build pipelines.
\par Analysts use SQL and BI tools.
\par Data scientists explore data and develop models.
\par ML engineers deploy and monitor models.
\par They work through the Databricks Data Intelligence Platform using technologies such as Spark, Delta Lake, MLflow, and Unity Catalog.
\par The platform connects them to a shared, governed data foundation on cloud storage.
\par The purpose is to improve collaboration and reduce disconnected data workflows.\'94
\par \~
\par Slide 13: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Databricks core commponents}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide introduces five foundational Databricks components.
\par The Workspace supports collaborative development, jobs, SQL, and dashboards.
\par Apache Spark and Photon process data at scale.
\par Delta Lake provides reliable tables with transactions and schema controls.
\par MLflow manages the machine-learning and AI lifecycle.
\par Unity Catalog provides governance, access control, auditing, and lineage.
\par Together, these components provide development, compute, transactional data management, AI lifecycle management, and governance.\'94
\par \~
\par Slide 14: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Key benefits of databricks}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide brings together the main reasons organizations consider Databricks.
\par First, it gives engineers, analysts, and AI teams a more connected place to work instead of making them move constantly between isolated tools.
\par It also uses open technologies and formats, which makes the data more accessible to other tools. That reduces dependency, although it does not remove every form of vendor or cloud dependency.
\par Compute can scale according to demand, so teams do not always need to maintain the same amount of capacity.
\par People can share notebooks, code, data, and results through common workspaces.
\par Unity Catalog helps answer important questions: Who can access this data? Where did it come from? And now how the data being used?
\par Finally, storage and compute can scale separately, giving organizations more control over spending.
\par \~
\par Slide 15: In next set of slide we will talk about databricks architechture 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide shows the high-level Databricks architecture.
\par Users connect through the UI, notebooks, APIs, SQL clients, and BI tools.
\par The Databricks-manages compute and control plane.
\par  control plane handles workspace services, orchestration, compute management, and governance metadata.
\par The compute plane processes the data.
\par Classic compute runs in the customer\rquote s cloud account. Serverless compute is managed by Databricks.
\par In a typical Spark cluster, the driver coordinates the job and workers execute tasks.
\par Data is stored separately, commonly as Delta Lake tables in cloud object storage.
\par This separation enables independent management of 
\par platform services, compute, and storage.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 16: How Apache spark runs a job }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide shows what happens after we write Spark code.
\par First, we describe the transformations we want, such as filtering, joining, or grouping data. 
\par Spark usually waits until we request an actual result before it begins the work.
\par The driver then looks at the complete request and builds an execution plan. It breaks that plan into stages and smaller tasks.
\par The cluster manager provides the computing resources, 
\par and executor processes it and run on the worker machines. 
\par The driver sends tasks to those executors, 
\par which process those different data partitions at the same time.
\par The output might appear in our notebook, return as a small result, or be written to a Delta table.
\par Why is this important? Spark becomes powerful by dividing a large problem into smaller pieces that can run in parallel. But simply adding more executors is not always enough; the data and workload must also be organized for effective parallel processing.
\'94
\par In Databricks:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\kerning2\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 1.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls1\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid11797960 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 You write Python, SQL, Scala, or R code in a notebook.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\kerning2\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 2.\tab}The Spark driver creates an execution plan.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\kerning2\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 3.\tab}Spark divides the data into partitions.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\kerning2\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 4.\tab}Workers process the partitions in parallel.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\kerning2\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 5.\tab}The output is displayed or written to a table.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid11797960 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \~
\par \~
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 17: Delta lake : reliability on the data lake }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93A regular data lake can contain many files, but files alone do not automatically behave like a reliable database table. Delta Lake helps solve that problem.
\par The actual records are stored mainly in Parquet files. 
\par Delta Lake then adds the _delta_log, 
\par which keeps track of valid files, table structure, and committed changes.
\par This log gives us several useful capabilities.
\par Transactions ensure users do not see half-completed updates. 
\par Time travel lets us inspect earlier table versions, as long as the required history is still retained.
\par Schema enforcement helps stop incorrectly structured data from entering the table. 
\par When the structure genuinely needs to change, schema evolution provides a controlled way to update it.
\par The same table can also support both scheduled batch processing and continuous streaming.
\par Why is this important? Delta Lake keeps the flexibility of cloud storage while making the data more reliable and manageable.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 18: clusters : the engines you turn on and off }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide explains Databricks compute options available to us .
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \'93This slide explains where the actual processing power in Databricks comes from.
\par For exploration and development, we can use interactive compute. We start it, connect a notebook, test code, 
\par and continue using it while we work. 
\par Auto-termination can be switched off after a period of inactivity.
\par For scheduled pipelines, job compute is usually a better fit. 
\par Databricks starts the resources for the job run and shuts them down when the run is complete.
\par Autoscaling allows classic compute to move between a minimum and maximum number of workers. 
\par It can add capacity during demanding periods and remove workers when less capacity is needed.
\par We can also choose between classic and serverless compute. 
\par Classic gives us more control over infrastructure. 
\par Serverless removes much of that management because Databricks provisions and scales the resources.
\par Why does this matter? The best compute choice depends on whether the workload is interactive, scheduled, highly customized, or designed for minimal infrastructure management.\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 \~
\par \~
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 19: Unity catalog : one layer of security & governance}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide answers a simple but important question: how do we keep data controlled and understandable as more people begin using it?
\par Unity Catalog organizes assets using three levels. We start with a catalog, then a schema, and finally the actual object. So for example here main is the catalog, sales is the schema, and orders is the table or view.
\par Unity Catalog then adds governance around those objects.
\par It helps control who can access data and, where needed, which rows or columns they can see.
\par When comes to data lineage , It records supported lineage, showing where data came from and which reports, jobs, or tables depend on it.
\par Audit records , it help us to investigate who accessed or changed governed assets and when.
\par Search and discovery it helps users to find trusted information without relying on tribal knowledge.
\par This slide matters because data becomes more useful when people can find it, understand it, and use it securely.\'94
\par \~
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 Slide 20: End to end from raw source to insight }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid11797960\charrsid11797960 
\par \~
\par \'93This slide puts everything we\rquote ve discussed into one picture.
\par We begin on the left with data coming from databases, applications, files, sensors, or streaming systems.
\par The first destination is usually the Bronze layer. Here, we preserve the data close to its original form so that we retain history and can reprocess it later if needed.
\par Next is Silver. This is where we clean the data, remove duplicates, correct types, handle errors, and combine related sources.
\par Gold is where the data becomes easier for the business to use. It may contain sales summaries, customer metrics, operational KPIs, or datasets prepared for analytics and AI.
\par  Databricks platform performs the work,
\par  Delta Lake keeps the tables reliable, and Unity Catalog controls and tracks the governed assets.
\par Why is this slide important? It shows that an insight is not created in one step. It is the result of an entire trusted data journey.\'94
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid744158 
